<div class="page">
    <div class="section-title">6. U. T. San Antonio: Discussion and appropriate action regarding proposed revisions to Mission Statement</div>
    <div class="section-title">RECOMMENDATION</div>
    <p>The Chancellor concurs with the recommendation of the Executive Vice Chancellor for Academic Affairs and President Romo that proposed changes to the U. T. San Antonio Mission Statement as set forth below be approved by the U. T. System Board of Regents and forwarded to the Texas Higher Education Coordinating Board for approval.</p>
    <div class="section-title">Proposed Revised Mission Statement</div>
    <p>The University of Texas at San Antonio is dedicated to the advancement of knowledge through research and discovery, teaching and learning, community engagement and public service. As an institution of access and excellence, UTSA embraces multicultural traditions, serving as a center for intellectual and creative resources as well as a catalyst for socioeconomic development for Texas, the nation and the world.</p>
    <div class="section-title">Current Mission Statement</div>
    <p>The University of Texas at San Antonio is the premier public institution of higher education in South Texas, with a growing national and international reputation. Renowned as an institution of access and excellence at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, UTSA is committed to research and discovery, teaching and learning, and public service. UTSA embraces the multicultural traditions of South Texas, serves as a center for intellectual and creative resources, and is a catalyst for the economic development of Texas.</p>
    <div class="section-title">BACKGROUND INFORMATION</div>
    <p>In Spring 2006, U. T. San Antonio began a year-long strategic planning process, labeled UTSA 2016. This process involved a core group of 80 faculty, staff, students, administrators, and community members; nine thematic planning groups from across U. T. San Antonio that reported to the core planning group; and the Silver to Gold Commission, selected members of U. T. San Antonio's external community. These groups met regularly throughout the year to develop the plan. Additionally, over 20 meetings were held with constituent groups across the University community throughout the process to facilitate dialogue and gather input.</p>
    <p>Part of this strategic planning process involved developing vision and core values statements and revising U. T. San Antonio's mission statement. As dialogue took place about the kind of institution U. T. San Antonio is now and hopes to become, participants were passionate about the fact that both research and access should continue to be emphasized in the mission statement. Participants also agreed that although U. T. San</p>
    <p style="text-align: right;">18</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div class="section-title">7. U. T. Austin: Recommendation regarding an honorific naming of the College of Engineering and retitling of the College to School</div>
    <div class="section-title">RECOMMENDATION</div>
    <p>President Powers and Dean Ben Streetman will make a recommendation concerning an honorific naming for the College of Engineering and retitling of the College to a School.</p>
    <p>If the recommendation is approved, the General Counsel to the Board will update the Regents' Rules and Regulations, Series 40601, concerning entities comprising the U. T. System institutions.</p>
    <div class="section-title">8. U. T. System: Briefing on proposal to revise Accountability and Performance framework and reporting schedule</div>
    <p>Dr. Geri H. Malandra, Vice Chancellor for Strategic Management, will brief the Board on proposed changes to the U. T. System Accountability and Performance framework and reporting schedule attached on Pages 20 - 26.</p>
    <p style="text-align: right;">19</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div class="section-title">Revised UT System Accountability and Reporting Framework</div>
    <p>The UT System has published a comprehensive, annual accountability and performance report for the past four years. The use of these reports at the system and campus level, together with the publication in August 2006 of a new ten-year strategic plan for the UT System, and the presence of more robust accountability data sets at the System and at the Texas Higher Education Coordinating Board (THECB), will make it possible for the UT System to focus and streamline its future accountability and performance reports.</p>
    <div class="section-title">A revised accountability framework for the UT System would provide a focus on:</div>
    <ol>
        <li>Outcomes, analysis, and metrics that match System priorities in its new strategic plan.</li>
        <li>"At-a-glance" views of trends for key metrics.</li>
        <li>Progress tracking and in-depth analysis of key initiatives and correlations among indicators, on graduation rates, cost of education, learning outcomes, health care, and other critical topics.</li>
        <li>Institution-specific profiles with in-depth analysis of trends in comparison with peer institutions.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="section-title">This framework assumes a reliance on a robust, unit-record set of data in a statistical source book that would be used to create the higher level analyses and correlations in the accountability report, together with robust data sets collected by the THECB. It also assumes a distinction between accountability and strategic management of all initiatives, with an annual cycle of in-depth reports to the Board on special topics, and annual updates on progress toward overall strategic plan goals (schedule attached on pp. 3-6).</div>
    <div class="section-title">Proposed framework</div>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>System trends.</strong> Would provide data on core, strategically key indicators, with an "at-a-glance" System-level tables and graphs.</li>
        <li><strong>Strategic initiatives.</strong> Would present discussion of progress on System-level initiatives and priorities such as the competitiveness initiative, productivity and efficiency initiatives such as shared services, and financial and information security planning.</li>
        <li><strong>For academic institution priorities, progress would be summarized on:</strong> enrollment management; community college partnerships; admissions standards; tuition-setting, cost, and financial aid; graduation rates; STEM degree production; and learning outcomes.</li>
        <li><strong>For health institutions, progress would be summarized on:</strong> improving health care, medical innovations, diversity, and professional and high-priority degrees.</li>
        <li><strong>Institution profiles.</strong> "Snapshot," including the short mission statement and top five priorities from the Compact and a statistical profile.</li>
        <li><strong>Analysis section focusing on analysis of data trends from a 360-degree, cross-tabbed perspective versus a metric-by-metric approach.</strong> Focus would be on more extensive analysis and context, for example, how tuition-setting, graduation rates initiatives, enrollment management plans, etc., all work together at each institution.</li>
        <li><strong>Tables and graphs on campus-relevant metrics and trends.</strong></li>
        <li><strong>Overall peer comparisons (based on campuses' current peer lists) will be included, as well as other national trends or examples.</strong></li>
    </ul>
    <div class="section-title">Proposed timeline</div>
    <ul>
        <li>February 2008: Fast Facts accountability trends summary and update on strategic plan initiatives</li>
        <li>May 2008: New Accountability and Performance Report</li>
        <li>May - December 2007: Separate, in-depth reports on special topics</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="text-align: right;">20</p>
</div>

<div class="page">
    <div class="section-title">Index of Performance Measures</div>
    <div class="section-title">A. Student Access, Success, and Outcomes</div>
    <ul>
        <li>Preparation & Academic Cost</li>
        <li>Ethnic composition of first-time undergraduates compared with composition of high school graduates in state</li>
        <li>Number and percent of first-time undergraduates from top 10% of their high school class, and by ethnicity</li>
